In article <43805@hpcndm.CND.HP.COM> neal@hpcndm.CND.HP.COM (Neal Bauer) writes:

>Some of the suggestions for modifying spell casting are great.  But I'm
>struck that people want to keep "lesser intelligent" characters from
>being able to cast the more powerful spells, yet don't complain that
>the more intelligent characters can easily "hack-n-slash" with the
>best of them.  
>

It shouldn't be impossible for the "lesser-intelligent" characters to be able
to cast spells, just very hard.  Equally spell-casters find it very difficult
(at least in the beginning) to "hack-n-slash" ; I know, I tried with a Wizard,
and I didn't last long :)
Of course, as time goes by, spell-users become better at fighting, through
finding and buying better weapons, armour etc ; and conversely, the better
warriors find lots of magic thingies.  Nethack is very powerful in magic and
as such, it is easier to survive at the early levels as a hack n slash type
then as an "intellectual spell-caster".  However, in you can survive with a
Wizard past the first few levels, then he/she becomes much more powerful. This
sounds about OK, dare I suggest that "hack-n-slash" types are of use for 
beginners to the game ; and when some skill and finesse is gained, the players
turn to characters requiring these.  Obviously, this does not cover all char-
types ; I enjoy playing Samurai characters, which are not total hack 'n slash,
but which dont require too much strategy at the early stages (ie running away )
:) 

>Finding gauntlets of power instantly maximizes your strength.  I think 
>it should work more like a helm of brilliance.  For crying out loud, even 
>if spell casting were made a more integral part of the game as suggested, 
>if I had a wizard with strength 25 and a +8 Mjollnir, I wouldn't NEED to 
>bother with spells.  I think that modifications need to be made so that a 
>wizard MUST rely on wizardry.  As the game stands, I find being a wizard 
>easiest.
>

There is nothing wrong with this. What is wrong is the overabundance of
magic items designed to strengthen your character as compared to those
to strengthen the intellect.  There is no reason a Wizard should not use 
a sword if threatened physically, however there is also no reason a Barbarian
cannot learn spells given time.  There should be certain items which a Wiz
cannot use   ("This item is beyond your power to master"), but which non magic
characters can.  Therefore, spell books could also be graded (as in M*ria) from
level I upwards...non spell-casters can only learn these in a certain pre-set
order, over a certain time period, dependant upon their class and the level.

>Neal

This has become long and convoluted, I glimpse an idea here, I will go away
and think about it.  See you all later :)

"Oh wow!  Everything looks so cosmic!"

Stew

